# Backlog Workflow

This project uses the LeanVibes backlog workflow with GitHub Issues and Projects.

## Fix Now or File? (default: fix now)

Fix a defect (a bug, a broken doc or check) in this session, as its own PR,
when all of these hold:
- The cause is confirmed (a failing test, a repro, or the code itself), not a hypothesis.
- The fix is small and needs no decision from the owner.
- It won't derail the current task.

Present a backlog suggestion instead when any of these hold:
- It needs the owner's decision or a priority call
- It has to wait (a date, data accumulating) - park it with a `waiting:*` label
- It spans several PRs
- The current task must finish first
- The cause isn't understood yet - record what's known vs. guessed
- It is scope expansion: work tangential to the task, an enhancement rather than
  a requirement, technical debt, or something the user said to do "later"

Suggestion format:
```
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|  BACKLOG SUGGESTION                                      |
|                                                          |
|  "[Brief description of the work]"                       |
|                                                          |
|  [Why this should be filed, not done now]                |
|                                                          |
|  - Type: [feature/bug/enhancement/debt]                  |
|  - Suggested Priority: [high/medium/low]                 |
|                                                          |
|  Reply with:                                             |
|  - "do it" to implement now                              |
|  - "backlog" to add to backlog                           |
|  - "backlog [priority]" to add with specific priority    |
+----------------------------------------------------------+
```

## Adding to Backlog

When user says "backlog" or "add to backlog", first check for an existing issue
(`gh issue list --search "<terms>"`), then create a GitHub Issue:

```bash
gh issue create \
  --title "[title]" \
  --body "## Summary
[Description]

## Context
Added during Cursor session. [What was seen, where (file paths), known vs. guessed]

Introduced by: PR #[N]   <- bugs only: the PR that caused it, or 'unknown'

## Why now
[Optional: what it risks or blocks; omit if nothing to say]

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] [Criteria based on the work]
- [ ] Verify implementation works as expected
- [ ] No regressions introduced

---
*Created via LeanVibes backlog workflow*" \
  --label "backlog" \
  --label "priority:[high/medium/low]" \
  --label "type:[feature/bug/enhancement/debt]"
```

Then add to the project board:
```bash
PROJECT_NUM=$(gh project list --owner @me --format json 2>/dev/null | jq -r '[.projects[] | select(.title=="Backlog") | .number] | first // empty')
[ -n "$PROJECT_NUM" ] && gh project item-add "$PROJECT_NUM" --owner @me --url [issue URL]
```

## Viewing Backlog

When user asks to see the backlog, list **every** open issue (the `backlog` label
only records which tool filed it; don't filter on it):

```bash
gh issue list --state open --limit 500 --json number,title,labels,body
```

If exactly 500 come back, say the list may be cut off.

Group each issue into the first match:
1. **Parked**: any `waiting:*` label, or `stays open`
2. **Epics**: label `epic`
3. **Workable**: everything else, shown by priority (high, medium, low, unprioritized)

For `waiting:date` and `waiting:issue` issues, read the first line of the latest
comment that starts with `Parked:`. Show as **due** a `Parked: waiting:date until YYYY-MM-DD`
whose date has passed, and as **may be unblocked** a `Parked: waiting:issue on #N`
whose #N is closed. Recommend work from Workable and due issues only.

## Parking an Issue

When an issue can't move until something outside the work changes, add one label
and a comment whose first line has this exact format:

| Label | First line of the comment |
|---|---|
| `waiting:date` | `Parked: waiting:date until YYYY-MM-DD` |
| `waiting:decision` | `Parked: waiting:decision` (question on the next line) |
| `waiting:issue` | `Parked: waiting:issue on #N` |
| `waiting:external` | `Parked: waiting:external` (who and what on the next line) |

Remove the label when the wait ends. Don't park an issue just because it's large.

## Working on Backlog Items

When user asks to work on an issue, fetch it with its recent comments:

```bash
gh issue view [number] --json title,body,labels,comments
```

If it has a `waiting:*` label, say what it is waiting on and confirm before starting.
If it is `stays open` or `epic`, ask which specific task under it to do.

Before editing files, if other AI sessions may be active, work in a per-issue
worktree branched from the remote default branch (never from the current HEAD):

```bash
git fetch origin
git worktree add .claude/worktrees/[number] -b fix/[number]-[slug] origin/main
```

Display:
```
Working on #[number]: [title]
Commits will include: Fixes #[number]
```

## Commit Messages

When working on a backlog item, always include the issue reference:

```
[Commit message]

Fixes #[number]
```

## Pull Requests

Before opening a PR: keep it under ~600 added lines outside tests/docs (split
otherwise), and run the CI workflow's lint/test steps locally.

Include in the description:

```
Closes #[number]
```

For an issue that must stay open across several PRs, use `Refs #[number]` and keep
`fix: #[number]` out of the PR title - GitHub closes issues from the squash commit's
title too.
